@startuml 03-sequence-immediate
' =====================================================================
' A submission run, with IMMEDIATE targets graded inline.
' Traces to: FR-19..FR-28, FR-32..FR-36
'
' Two things to note about ownership:
'  * The instructor's autograder script drives both phases. It runs her
'    test suite, then invokes our AI phase. We do not own run_autograder.
'  * Her suite calls US. We never enumerate targets and invoke tests.
' =====================================================================

title Submission Run -- IMMEDIATE targets graded inline

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam maxMessageSize 220

actor Student as stud
participant "Gradescope" as gs #E8E8E8
box "Autograder Container" #F7F7F7
  participant "Autograder Script\n(run_autograder)" as script #FFF2CC
  participant "Instructor\nTest Suite" as ti #FFF2CC
  participant "Reporting\nLibrary" as wrap #FBE6D4
  participant "AI Grading\nPhase" as aip #D6E4F0
end box
participant "Web API" as api #DCEFD9
database "Grading DB" as db #DCEFD9
participant "LLM\nProviders" as llm #E8E8E8

stud -> gs : submit code
gs -> script : run_autograder

note over script #FFF2CC
  The instructor owns this script. It invokes
  her test suite, then invokes our AI phase as a
  separate command. Ordering and error handling
  are hers.
end note

== Phase 1: functional testing. Her suite reports to us. ==

script -> ti : run the test suite

loop for each test the instructor wrote
  ti -> ti : run the assertion
  ti -> wrap : record_outcome(target,\ntest_name, status, output)
  wrap -> api : POST /outcomes\n{platform_submission_id, submitted_at,\n target, test_name, status, output}
  api -> db : upsert submission\n(idempotent on platform_submission_id)
  api -> db : insert test_outcome
  api --> wrap : 200
end

note right of wrap #FFF8DC
  **Unknown target names fail loudly.** The API
  rejects an identifier not registered on this
  assignment with a 4xx naming it and listing
  the registered ones, and the library raises
  rather than swallowing it. A typo must fail
  the instructor's test run, never record an
  outcome against nothing.
end note

opt her marking logic decides this target needs feedback
  ti -> wrap : request_ai_feedback(target)
  wrap -> api : POST /ai-requests {target}
  api -> db : record the request\n(honoured only if policy\n= AUTOGRADER)
  api --> wrap : 200
  note right of ti #FFF8DC
    Under AUTOGRADER this call IS the decision.
    Failures only, a subset, a superset, a
    partial-credit threshold: all just code she
    writes here. That is why no ON_FAILURE
    policy exists.
  end note
end

ti --> script : suite exits

== Phase 2: the autograder script invokes the AI phase ==

script -> aip : invoke AI phase
aip -> api : POST /submissions/{id}/resolve-marks
api -> db : for each REGISTERED target,\nresolve ai_needed:\nALWAYS -> true, NEVER -> false,\nAUTOGRADER -> was it requested
api -> db : insert grading_task rows

alt a registered target has no recorded outcome
  api --> aip : 409 {missing_targets[]}
  aip --> script : exit non-zero with a diagnostic
  note right of aip #FFF8DC
    An error, not a silent skip. The message must
    say the assignment is misconfigured and the
    student need do nothing, because on
    Gradescope the student sees it too.
  end note
else all registered targets reported
  api --> aip : 200 {tasks[], each with\nfeedback_mode,\nai_graded_submitted_at}
end

note over aip #FFF8DC
  The guard is checked even here. An instructor
  or TA re-running the autograder later must not
  re-bill the LLM.
end note

loop for each task with ai_needed AND feedback_mode = IMMEDIATE
  aip -> api : GET /tasks/{id}/request
  api -> db : SELECT rubric, guidance,\ntest_outcome, submission_file
  api --> aip : 200 {FeedbackRequest}
  aip -> aip : build prompt\n(rubric + guidance +\noutcomes + code)

  group Multi-provider fan-out [FR-23, FR-27]
    par
      aip -> llm : Claude: generate
      llm --> aip : text, suggested_score
    else
      aip -> llm : ChatGPT: generate
      llm --> aip : text, suggested_score
    end
    aip -> aip : compare, estimate confidence,\nselect one
  end

  aip -> api : POST /tasks/{id}/feedback\n{responses[], comparison, selected}
  api -> db : insert feedback (one row per provider),\ninsert feedback_comparison,\nUPDATE ai_status = DONE
  api --> aip : 201
end

note right of aip #FFF8DC
  NIGHTLY tasks are skipped here and stay
  NEEDED. That is what puts this submission in
  tonight's trigger queue, with no extra
  bookkeeping.

  If every target was IMMEDIATE, the guard is
  set below and this submission is done in one
  write. If any was NIGHTLY, results.json is
  written again after tonight's rerun, and the
  packager must emit placeholders for the
  pending targets so the student does not read
  silence as success.
end note

aip -> api : POST /submissions/{id}/ai-complete
api -> db : UPDATE submission\nSET ai_graded_submitted_at = submitted_at\nWHERE NO task is still NEEDED
api --> aip : 200 {guard_set}

aip -> api : GET /submissions/{id}/feedback
api -> db : SELECT feedback WHERE is_selected
api --> aip : 200 {items[], total_score}
aip -> aip : package results.json,\nwith placeholders for any\ntask still NEEDED [FR-35]
aip --> script : results.json
script -> gs : write results.json
gs --> stud : test results, feedback for\nIMMEDIATE targets,\nand "pending" for NIGHTLY ones

note over aip, llm #FFF8DC
  **Constraint specific to IMMEDIATE targets.**
  This all happens inside the platform's
  autograder timeout, and submissions spike
  before a deadline. Targets times providers
  times LLM latency adds up.

  Per-target mode helps: mark the one or two
  targets worth instant feedback as IMMEDIATE
  and leave the rest NIGHTLY. Also consider
  capping IMMEDIATE targets to a single provider
  and using multi-provider comparison only at
  night.
end note

@enduml
